# Parameter validation

When a method needs to validate arguments, put **guard clauses at the beginning** of the method body — before any other logic.

Add validation only when there is a real contract to enforce (null references, empty strings, invalid ranges, etc.). Do not add redundant checks on every private helper.

## Preferred style

Use `Argument.ThrowIfNull` from Common:

```csharp
public void Process(string path, IReadOnlyList<Item> items)
{
    Argument.ThrowIfNull(path);
    Argument.ThrowIfNullOrEmpty(items);

    // method logic...
}
```

## Avoid

Do **not** validate via assignment with null-coalescing throw:

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
path = path ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(path));
this._path = path ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(path));
```

Do **not** bury validation mid-method or inline it in expressions/assignments.

## Exception choice

- `Argument.ThrowIfNull` / `ArgumentNullException` — null reference where a value is required
- `ArgumentException` — non-null but invalid (empty string, out of range, unknown enum value, etc.)

Use `nameof(parameter)` for non-null validation exception parameter names.
